  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1152 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nA slightly cooler, but noticeably drier air mass in the wake of\nyesterday's cold front will limit instability across the\nMidsouth. As a result, we will not see the afternoon showers and\nthunderstorms that were prevalent across much of the region\nyesterday.\n\nThe break from oppressive heat will be short-lived. A strong\nridge currently over the Plains will shift over the Mississippi\nand Ohio River Valleys Tuesday. The Bermuda High positioned along\nthe East Coast and relatively low pressure along the lee side of\nthe Rockies will set up a southerly flow along the Mississippi\nRiver Valley early tomorrow. As a result, moisture levels will\ntrend higher, resulting in higher heat indices. Temperatures will\nalso tick up a degree or two as the ridge builds, resulting in\nthe return of 105+ degree heat indices across portions of the Mid-\nSouth. A Heat Advisory has been issued for portions of southwest \nTennessee, east-central Arkansas, and northwest Mississippi \ntomorrow, with an expansion of that area anticipated Wednesday. A \ndry air mass will limit any precipitation across the Mid-South \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nThe ridge over the Ohio and Mississippi River Valleys will weaken\nand flatten out Wednesday.  Zonal flow will set up across the\nnorthern and central plains, across the Great Lakes and Ohio\nValley. Late Wednesday into Thursday a trough associated with a\ndeep low over Canada will swing across the Great Lakes. An\nattendant cold front will approach the Mid-South early Thursday.\nCurrent WPC rainfall totals for this system do not look\nimpressive, but we can use every drop as drought conditions\nworsen across the Mid-South. Details regarding exact rainfall\namounts and potential for any strong to severe storms will come\ninto better focus over the next couple of days, but overall\nexpect slightly cooler temperatures and at least a gambler's\nchance of rain Thursday and Friday. The weekend looks drier and\nwarmer as a ridge builds back in from the west.",
